    %main.sub-main
      .sub-content
        %h1= archart_h1
        %p.sub-lead= archart_lead

        %h2= archart_h2_pipeline
        %p
          PteronautOS is an ExpressLRS 4.x fork that replaced the "PWM passthrough" tail of the
          receiver with a dedicated flight-control layer. The data path is deliberately short:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              CRSF radio link
                │  2.4 GHz SX1280
                ▼
              rx_main / CRSF decoder        → ChannelData[]  (raw 172…1811 µs-window values)
                ▼
              Ornithopter::update()          → _readChannels() → voice* fields
                ▼  kernel select: activeProfile ≥ GEARBOX_2VTAIL_1RUD ?
              _computeServoMixer()   OR   _computeGearboxMixer()
                ▼
              _f[]  (indexed by ServoFunc tag, µs)
                ▼
              PWM output stage               → funcMap channels → wing / rudder / motor servos
        %p
          Four structural ideas keep the beast stable and the code small:
        %ol
          %li
            <strong>Channels are normalized early.</strong> Every raw value in the 172–1811 CRSF
            window is mapped to −1…+1 (<code>_crsfToNorm</code>) or to a percentage once, and only
            that unit flows through the mixers — no repeated magic constants.
          %li
            <strong>Outputs are tagged, not pinned.</strong> The mixers write into
            <code>_f[ServoFunc]</code>. Which physical PWM output a tag drives is decided later by
            the profile's <code>funcMap</code> — see the
            <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}documentation/mixers/">Mixer Reference</a>.
            Re-pinning a model never touches mixer math.
          %li
            <strong>The kernel is a compile-time family, a runtime choice.</strong> Both the
            waveform flapper and the gearbox controller live in the binary; the active profile
            picks the kernel every update tick.
          %li
            <strong>Failsafe is not an event, it is a state.</strong> Link-down, bench mode and
            stick-override are explicit gates in <code>update()</code>, so servos can never run
            away while the radio is silent.

        %h2= archart_h2_kernels
        %p
          A <em>kernel</em> is the block that turns normalized channels into servo-function µs.
          PteronautOS has two families, discriminated by the mixer-profile number:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Family
            %th Profiles
            %th Actuators
            %th Model archetype
          %tr
            %td
              %strong Waveform (servo) kernel
            %td 0 SERVO_2WING · 1 SERVO_2WING_1RUD · 2 SERVO_4WING
            %td Flapping wing servos (2–4), crest rudder
            %td Direct-drive servo ornithopter — the classic PteronautOS
          %tr
            %td
              %strong Gearbox kernel
            %td 3 GEARBOX_2VTAIL_1RUD · 4 GEARBOX_1MOT_2VTAIL · 5 GEARBOX_1MOT_2VTAIL_1RUD · 6 GEARBOX_1ELE_1RUD · 7 GEARBOX_1MOT_1ELE_1RUD
            %td Motor ESC, V-tail or elevator + rudder
            %td Geared / motorised flapper with conventional tail surfaces
        %p
          Selection happens in <code>update()</code> with the single test
          <code>PROFILE_IS_GEARBOX</code> (<code>activeProfile &ge; 3</code>). The profile itself is
          a runtime value: it starts from the <code>MIXER_PROFILE</code> build flag (legacy
          <code>ORNITHOPTER_GEARBOX=1</code> still maps to profile&nbsp;5) but can be switched live
          from the WebUI via <code>setOrnithopterProfile()</code> — the firmware re-targets a new
          airframe without a reflash. There are eight profiles in total; their exact
          <code>funcMap</code> layouts are tabulated in the Mixer Reference.

        %h2= archart_h2_servo
        %p
          The waveform kernel is what most people mean by "PteronautOS": two wing servos run a
          phase-continuous oscillation whose shape is sculpted every cycle. Its anatomy:
        %ul
          %li
            <strong>Flap gate with hysteresis.</strong> Throttle must cross the flap threshold
            (default raw ≈303, ≈1.08&nbsp;ms PWM-equivalent) to enter flapping; it leaves only
            below threshold − 50 hysteresis, so marginal throttle never chatters the wings.
          %li
            <strong>Frequency.</strong> A FlappingOscillator advances with real elapsed time
            (µs clock, dt capped at 100&nbsp;ms). The cadence target blends the CH6 flap-frequency
            channel and throttle via the profile's <code>throttleFrequencyMix</code>, within
            0.5&nbsp;Hz…<code>flapBaseFreq</code>/10.
          %li
            <strong>Amplitude.</strong> Throttle percentage of the servo-speed ceiling
            <code>ampMax = degPerSec / (2·f)</code> (with <code>degPerSec = 60°/servoSpeed_ms</code>),
            hard-capped at 55° pre-multiplier. Amplitude therefore collapses naturally at high
            frequency — the wing never demands more than the servo can deliver.
          %li
            <strong>Ferocity.</strong> Down-stroke (power) and up-stroke (recovery) ferocities are
            shaped per stroke, modulated asymmetrically by elevator (pull-up hardens the downstroke,
            push-over hardens the upstroke) and by throttle coupling, then bounded 0…8 before the
            shape function.
          %li
            <strong>Yaw differentials.</strong> Rudder adds L/R-opposed ferocity
            (<code>rudderFerocityRange</code>) and L/R-opposed amplitude
            (<code>rudderAmplitudeDifferential</code>) while the stroke-reversal phase stays
            <em>shared</em> between the wings — the turn authority comes from asymmetric dwell, not
            from desynchronised wings.
          %li
            <strong>Geometry &amp; output.</strong> The shaped −1…+1 wave is scaled into degrees,
            mixed against aileron/elevator steering (±60° authority × scale), offset by the
            glide/flap-centre trim, clamped 0…180°, then converted to µs through the configurable
            <code>servoMinUs…servoMaxUs</code> envelope (988…2012 defaults) with per-function trim.
          %li
            <strong>Glide branch.</strong> Below the flap threshold the oscillator decays, and the
            wings hold a static glide angle — an ideal bench-and-launch state.
        %p
          The servo kernel therefore produces <em>pure wingbeat</em>: it never runs a motor ESC, and
          its rudder (when present) is the head/crest rudder carrying yaw + a roll coupling.

        %h2= archart_h2_gearbox
        %p
          The gearbox kernel trades pure wingbeat for a motorised drivetrain plus conventional
          tail surfaces. Because a motor turns continuously, this kernel is stateless per tick —
          no oscillator — and instead:
        %ul
          %li
            maps throttle (armed) to a 1000…2000&nbsp;µs ESC command (<code>SF_MOTOR</code>), idle
            when disarmed;
          %li
            mixes the tail: V-tail profiles use an elevon blend (aileron+elevator / aileron−elevator),
            classic profiles use a separate elevator surface;
          %li
            lets Zephyrus (when enabled) inject <em>roll and pitch PID</em> corrections into the
            tail servos, clamped to ±250&nbsp;µs (<code>ZEPHYR_GEARBOX_CLAMP_US</code>) so a
            sensor glitch can never stall a servo;
          %li
            keeps the same rudder mixer and µs/trim/clamp envelope as the waveform kernel.
        %p
          One ornithopter rig can even carry both personalities: flapping launch with the waveform
          kernel, cruise on the gearbox — the runtime profile switch is that single line in
          <code>update()</code>.

        %h2= archart_h2_state
        %p
          <code>update()</code> is called on a fixed ~10&nbsp;ms cadence from the WiFi/servo loop and
          gates on three flags before any mixer runs:
        %table
          %tr
            %th State
            %th Condition
            %th Behaviour
          %tr
            %td Normal flight
            %td linkUp + armed channel live
            %td Full kernel output follows the sticks
          %tr
            %td Bench / panel mode
            %td %code benchMode (WebUI panel, no RC link)
            %td Neutral sticks are injected, throttle parked below the flap threshold — glide + trim edits are visible live
          %tr
            %td Stick override
            %td %code stickOverride (virtual channels)
            %td Firmware-injected channel values replace CRSF entirely (servo sweeps, tests)
          %tr
            %td Failsafe
            %td link lost (<code>onLinkDown</code> / <code>enterFailsafe</code>)
            %td All functions centre to 1500&nbsp;µs, oscillator reset, motor → min; CRSF packet loss also centres at RX level
        %p
          The centre-on-loss behaviour is duplicated in two layers on purpose: the CRSF stack
          failsafes the channel data, and the Ornithopter layer failsafes the <em>geometry</em>
          (including the oscillation state). A dead radio therefore leaves a gliding, controllable
          airframe — never a flailing one.

        %h2= archart_h2_map
        %table
          %tr
            %th Module
            %th Path
            %th Responsibility
          %tr
            %td Ornithopter layer
            %td
              %code src/lib/Ornithopter/
            %td Kernel select, mixer math, waveform oscillator, profiles, trim
          %tr
            %td Flight profiles &amp; constants
            %td
              %code src/lib/Ornithopter/OrnithopterConfig.h
            %td MixerProfile enum, ServoFunc tags, funcMap, all numeric defaults
          %tr
            %td Oscillator / shape
            %td
              %code src/lib/Ornithopter/OrnithopterWaveform.h
            %td Phase integration, ferocity shaping, reversal logic
          %tr
            %td Zephyrus (optional)
            %td
              %code src/lib/Zephyrus/
            %td MPU6050 + Mahony AHRS, dual PID, crest/tail correction
          %tr
            %td PWM output stage
            %td
              %code src/lib/ServoOutput/devServoOutput.cpp
            %td Writes µs synchronised to the CRSF tick
          %tr
            %td WebUI config
            %td
              %code src/lib/WIFI/devWIFI.cpp
            %td Runtime profile + tuning persistence (<code>/pteronautos</code>)
        %p
          The next layer down — exactly which waveform shape the numbers produce, and how every
          profile wires functions to PWM outputs — lives in the
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}documentation/mixers/">Mixer Reference</a>, and the
          servo-timing envelope is documented in the
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}documentation/servo-kernel/">Servo Kernel</a> article.
